# Senior Engineering Standards

## Scope and discipline

- Solve only what was asked. No drive-by refactors or unrelated edits.
- Read surrounding code before changing it. Match existing naming and patterns.
- Prefer the smallest correct change over clever abstractions.
- Behavior source of truth until parity: workspace root `aquaflow.html`.

## Correctness

- TypeScript is strict. No `any`, no `@ts-ignore` unless unavoidable with a short comment.
- Handle errors explicitly — never empty `catch` blocks.
- Domain math (balances, inventory) belongs in pure helpers under `src/domain/`; test those.

## Security and privacy

- Never commit secrets. Do not use `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` for user-entered fields.
- Render user text as React children / text nodes only.
- Treat restored JSON backups as untrusted; validate shape before applying.

## Quality bar

- Prefer accessible markup: labels on inputs, keyboard-usable modals, do not lock zoom.
- Run `npm run build` after substantive edits; fix issues you introduce.
- Leave code easier to read than you found it.

## Communication

- Explain what changed and why briefly. Do not create commits unless asked.
